# Quiet Signal — line-ending and binary policy.
#
# Goal: a clone is byte-identical no matter who made it, or on which platform.
# Text is stored as LF and checked out as LF, so a contributor's core.autocrlf
# setting cannot leak CRLF into the repository.
#
# These rules take precedence over core.autocrlf. On Windows that setting is
# frequently enabled system-wide, which is exactly the situation this file
# exists to neutralise.

# Default: text files are normalised to LF in the repository and checked out
# as LF. git auto-detects binary content and leaves it untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch scripts are the one deliberate exception — cmd.exe expects CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never normalise, never attempt a text diff.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.jar  binary
*.apk  binary
*.aab  binary

# Signing material: never normalise. (Also gitignored, see .gitignore.)
*.jks      binary
*.keystore binary
*.p12      binary
